WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.400
Step four files the fleet's coverage.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.679
The query takes the latest
record for each Windows device

3
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:09.163
and counts them by onboarding
status and sensor health state.

4
00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:12.774
It runs through Graph so the
result is a file you can keep.

5
00:00:12.774 --> 00:00:17.437
On the slide it's wrapped to fit; on
the page each command is one line.

6
00:00:17.437 --> 00:00:21.424
The guide's pass: every device
onboarded with an active sensor,

7
00:00:21.424 --> 00:00:23.300
or in the exception register.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.556
Learn doesn't list the exact
onboarding status strings,

9
00:00:26.556 --> 00:00:29.813
so the query groups by them
rather than filtering on one.

10
00:00:29.813 --> 00:00:34.199
Step five is Microsoft's detection
test, on the test device, under its

11
00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:39.212
change record, from an elevated Command
Prompt, exactly as Learn publishes it.

12
00:00:39.212 --> 00:00:42.187
Learn says an alert appears
in about ten minutes.

13
00:00:42.187 --> 00:00:44.213
One caution the guide records:

14
00:00:44.213 --> 00:00:49.723
Learn's page lists Windows Server versions
as supported, not Windows ten or eleven,

15
00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:52.801
so on a client a missing
alert is inconclusive;

16
00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:57.176
rely on the coverage query and
Module 2's network protection test.

17
00:00:57.176 --> 00:00:58.713
Step six backs it out.

18
00:00:58.713 --> 00:01:01.484
Nothing changed but the
alert the test raised:

19
00:01:01.484 --> 00:01:04.970
resolve that incident in the
test tenant, and disconnect.
